{"answer":"You can build personal knowledge graphs for better recommendations by using LLMs to extract and generate semantic user/entity profiles, then storing those profiles in (or aligning them with) a knowledge graph for propagation and matching.

One approach is to generate semantic profiles for KG entities with an LLM, integrate them into the KG, and then train with a preference-matching objective that aligns LLM-based and KG-based representations (SPiKE) [1]. Another approach is to convert a user’s conversational “strings” into structured preference triples (RDF-compliant, linked to identifiers) using lightweight LLMs, and then evaluate how the resulting PKG improves downstream recommendation (KG triple extraction pipeline) [2]. For session-based settings, you can also infer latent user personas from a heterogeneous KG (initialized using LLM-derived item embeddings) and incorporate those persona representations into recommendation and reranking (persona-driven SBRS) [3].

These differ in where the LLM is used (profile generation into an existing KG [1], triple extraction from text for PKG construction [2], or persona inference for session personalization grounded in a heterogeneous KG [3]) and in the recommendation setting they target (general KG/LLM recommenders [1] vs. PKG construction [2] vs. session-based personalization [3]).","grounded":true}
